date: 2026-08-28 (still "4.0.0" -- see the version warning below.  THE MERGE
      MEMORY FIX WE ASKED FOR, plus a locking change and two bugs.  No format
      change, no API change: verified here, not taken on trust.

      OUR 9.6GB VACUUM MEASUREMENT IS WHAT PROMPTED IT, and upstream says so:
      "A compaction held its whole output in memory... Downstream measured 9.6GB
      peak RSS for a 2M-record VACUUM, and the omission was mine: format 4 put
      the pointer array last precisely so this would not be necessary, and then
      neither writer took advantage of it."  Both writers now stream through a
      fixed 256KB sink with an incremental digest, so the only per-record state
      is an 8-byte offset (D-29).  A growing buffer was rejected deliberately --
      it would make the footprint a second function of the output.

      MEASURED HERE, AND THE FIX IS REAL BUT DOES NOT CLOSE IT.  2M records
      through SQL with VACUUM, process peak RSS, one pass per arm (RSS is
      deterministic to about 1MB across eight passes, so one is enough):

          rowid 100B          2245MB -> 1397MB   (-38%)
          WITHOUT ROWID 400B  13798MB -> 9824MB  (-29%)

      So a 2M WITHOUT ROWID VACUUM at 400-byte values still peaks near 10GB on
      this laptop.  Upstream's own arithmetic predicts "a 2M-record compaction
      of 60MB files is 140MB, being 60 in, 60 out and the 16MB pointer array" --
      which is nothing like what we see.  THE TWO NUMBERS ARE NOT MEASURING THE
      SAME THING and the difference should not be reported as a contradiction:
      ru_maxrss counts resident FILE-BACKED pages, and their figure is about
      ALLOCATION, which is the distinction their commit draws itself (zstool
      check reports 307MB in 0.02s having touched almost none of it).  Our shape
      also makes the database far larger than their fixture: a WITHOUT ROWID
      key IS the whole encoded record and the value is that record again.
      What is safe to say downstream: the scheduling constraint on VACUUM is
      REDUCED, not removed.  Re-measure on the ZFS box before deploying.

      NO FORMAT CHANGE -- VERIFIED AT OUR LAYER, not just asserted.  Upstream
      says the golden corpus is byte-identical; here, the old build and the new
      one each read the other's database (50000 rows, same length sum), both
      write magic digit '4', the in-order files come out the same size to the
      byte (6300276), and the payload between the header and the trailer is
      BYTE-IDENTICAL over 6.3MB.  Only the per-database UUID and the two
      checksums that cover it differ, which is exactly what should differ.

      NO API CHANGE: zeroskip.h, xxhash.h and zsbench.c are byte-identical to
      f7521ee; only zeroskip.c moved.  Checked the way the merge_memory miss
      taught us -- every zs_/ZS_ token our sources use, grepped against the
      incoming header.

      ALSO IN HERE, AND WORTH KNOWING BECAUSE IT IS A CONCURRENCY CHANGE:
      C-1m, a compaction of an already-packed set now takes the repack lock
      ALONE, so a concurrent writer keeps appending for its whole duration.
      D-19b replaces a per-tombstone search with one forward history cursor.
      And two bugs: zsi_convert_pending was only reachable through a successful
      active-file conversion, so a seal left a straggler mid-set and a later
      compaction merged nothing and returned ZS_BADFORMAT for a database that
      was merely behind on D-12 (D-25b); and a part-written staging file is now
      unlinked by both writers.  busy-test, backup-concurrent and twowriter all
      pass, which is where a lock change would show.

      Version note, not a problem: the Makefile still says 4.0.0 and the 4.0.0
      CHANGELOG entry was amended in place rather than a new version cut, so
      "4.0.0" names both f7521ee and 9e1a2ac.  Nothing ever shipped on 4.0.0
      anywhere, so no consumer can be confused by it and there is nothing to
      raise upstream.  Where it touches US is only this file and the doc, which
      now hold TWO production campaigns both labelled 4.0.0 -- and both are
      keyed by commit here, and prodrun/fmt4run record `vendored:` in env.txt,
      so that is already handled.  Keep quoting the commit, as we do.

      Test tier on this laptop, all green: zskey-test, zsbtree-test, the 10 SQL
      batteries, format-guard, crash-test 10 rounds, busy-test,
      backup-concurrent, and the TCL permutation.

source: f7521ee
date: 2026-08-27 (library 4.0.0 -- FORMAT 4.  An ABI break and an on-disk break.
      A version-4 build refuses a version-2 or version-3 file at open, there is
      no migrator, and existing data must be dumped and reloaded.

      FORMAT 3 WAS WITHDRAWN AND NEVER RELEASED.  Upstream went 2.9.2 -> 4.0.0,
      skipped the version number so the two layouts can never be confused, and
      says version 3 must never be written.  The files it refers to as existing
      "outside this repository" are OURS: everything this branch wrote between
      2026-08-21 and today.  What we vendored as 3.0.0/3.1.0/3.1.1 was that
      unreleased line; 3.1.1's xxhash work is in the released tree as 2.9.2.

      THE SHAPE.  Both file kinds now hold the same self-framing record: a
      4-bit flag nibble in the low half of byte 0, then keylen and vallen (4
      bytes of header while keylen <= 4095 and vallen <= 65535, 16 above that),
      then key NUL value NUL, PACKED rather than rounded up to a multiple of 8.
      An in-order file is [header][records][pad][pointers][trailer], the
      pointer array AFTER the data, a 32-byte trailer naming it and carrying
      one checksum over the whole file.  So key/value ADJACENCY is back, which
      is the property format 3 gave up and the one our production A/B said was
      costing us on fetch.

      THE API BREAK THAT REACHED US IS NOT THE ONE THEY FLAGGED.  Upstream
      warned about zs_csum returning uint64_t (external engines only -- we
      supply none) and ZS_NOCSUM being rejected rather than ignored (already
      deleted here at format 3).  Neither touched the build.  What did was
      merge_memory, REMOVED from zs_open_data -- unmentioned, because they diff
      4.0.0 against 2.9.2, which never had the field, and we were on the 3.1.x
      line that did.  Deleted here: btree_zs.c's uriMergeMemory, the URI
      parameter zs_merge_memory, and prodrun.sh's mergemem phase (deleted, not
      disabled -- SQLite ignores an unrecognised URI parameter silently, so the
      phase would have run three identical arms and printed a clean null).
      The bound is now structural: the pointer array last means a merge holds 8
      bytes per record in one pass.  It still does NOT bound a merge's mapped
      INPUTS, which are what made a 2M VACUUM peak at 1.2-1.4GB, so that figure
      is still a format-3 number until someone re-measures it.

      WHAT WE MEASURED, AND WHAT WE DID NOT.  On-disk size is deterministic and
      a laptop may decide it: 200k records, identical file counts in both arms,
      format 4 is -4.4% (rowid/100B), -2.3% (rowid/400B), -2.4% (WITHOUT
      ROWID/100B), -3.4% (WITHOUT ROWID/400B).  Upstream's -3.1% holds on our
      shapes.  THE READ COMPARISON IS OPEN and belongs on the ZFS box:
      test/zs/fmt2ab.sh is retargeted (guard demands magic digits '1' and '4';
      a '2' is the withdrawn format 3 and means a stale tree), and both arms
      now share the CURRENT src/btree_zs.c, because removing merge_memory left
      the old and new versions of that file differing only in comments and the
      dead zs_nocsum plumbing.  Only the library moves now.

      THE FORMAT-GUARD PREDICTION WAS WRONG AND THE SUITE PASSED UNCHANGED.
      Upstream expected the checked-in format-2 fixture to stop opening.  The
      refusal is per FILE: zs_db_open still skips a member it cannot decode, so
      a directory whose members are all refused is indistinguishable from an
      empty one to an open with ZS_CREATE, and the caller-visible answer is
      still an empty database.  The guard now proves that is the mechanism by
      putting the same file BESIDE a readable database, where it does surface
      as SQLITE_CORRUPT, and a fixtures/fmt3-db is checked in beside fmt2-db.

      AND THE HAZARD OUR OWN DOC HAD BACKWARDS.  A write into a superseded
      directory does not start a fresh generation beside the old data -- it
      reuses the same active-file name and TRUNCATES it (12712 bytes -> 277,
      schema gone).  A read is safe.  NOT new at version 4: the format-3 build
      destroys a format-2 directory identically, measured on both arms.  So
      "dump and reload" is an instruction to follow BEFORE upgrading, and
      format-guard.sh now asserts both halves.

      PRODUCTION (2026-08-28, stl-imap-09, 128K, 8 passes, guards green --
      reference cell drifted 4.4%, no cell tripped the instability check):
      FORMAT 4 WINS ALL 12 CELLS ON BOTH ROWS, fetch +6..79% and scan
      +3..56%.  The laptop had fetch OVERLAPPING in all 12; the ZFS transfer
      is untrustworthy in BOTH directions, having hidden a real win here
      after inventing one for format 3.

      THE SCAN WIN IS CONFOUNDED AND THE FETCH WIN IS NOT -- which is the
      answer owed upstream, and it corrects what a contaminated laptop run
      suggested.  Three arms (fmt2 verifying / fmt2 zs_nocsum=1 / fmt4):
      on SCAN, removed verification is worth +9..34% where it resolves and
      the LAYOUT a further +1..41%, so BOTH matter and anyone quoting the
      scan number against format 2 is quoting roughly half read-path
      verification they no longer do.  On FETCH, verification is overlap or
      +1..3% everywhere while LAYOUT carries +5..28% -- and +62..77% at
      WITHOUT ROWID/400B/2M.  A lookup verifies one record, a scan verifies
      every one.  The ab and attrib phases are independent runs and agree
      cell by cell on end-to-end fetch.

      COMPACTION WIDENS THE FETCH GAP (VACUUM=1, 2 files in both arms, so no
      D-14d confound): rowid/100B +7..11%, rowid/400B +20..27%, WITHOUT
      ROWID/100B +11..14%, WITHOUT ROWID/400B +99..103%.  Per LOOKUP, not
      per matched file, and the mechanism is NOT attributed -- that wants a
      profile.  Compaction is still worth +25..33% on fetch for BOTH formats.

      AND FORMAT 4 DOES NOT BOUND A COMPACTION'S MEMORY.  Peak RSS through a
      2M VACUUM is 2-7% BELOW format 2 -- a rounding error, not a bound:
      1394->1338MB (rowid/100B), 3718->3632 (rowid/400B), 3261->3029
      (WITHOUT ROWID/100B), 10400->9604 (WITHOUT ROWID/400B).  Streaming the
      merge OUTPUT was never the problem; merge_memory never covered the
      mapped INPUTS and removing it does not either.  A VACUUM of 2M WITHOUT
      ROWID at 400-byte values peaks at 9.6GB and takes 28-33s.  That is a
      scheduling constraint, not a format regression.

      Test tier on this laptop, all green: zskey-test, zsbtree-test, the 10 SQL
      batteries, format-guard (with the new cases, each mutation-checked),
      crash-test 10 rounds, busy-test, backup-concurrent, twowriter (6),
      metacache (8), and the TCL permutation at 966/966 clean files.

